  {
   "n": 14,
   "type": "MSQ",
   "section": "Technical",
   "tags": "Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +1 / -0",
   "html": "<p>Which of the following statements about lexical error handling is/are TRUE?</p>",
   "options": [
    {
     "key": "A",
     "html": "Lexical analyzers may use finite automata with error transitions to recognize malformed tokens."
    },
    {
     "key": "B",
     "html": "Skipping characters until a delimiter is found is a common lexical error recovery strategy."
    },
    {
     "key": "C",
     "html": "Lexical error recovery always guarantees syntactic correctness in later phases."
    },
    {
     "key": "D",
     "html": "A lookahead buffer in a lexical analyzer can delay token finalization to reduce misclassification."
    }
   ],
   "answer": [
    "A",
    "B",
    "D"
   ],
   "natRange": null,
   "answerText": "A, B, D",
   "marks": {
    "pos": 1,
    "neg": 0
   },
   "solution": "<p>(a, b, d)<br />\n(a) Error transitions in finite automata help recognize invalid token sequences.<br />\n(b) This is a commonly used strategy &mdash; skip till a whitespace, semicolon, or another known delimiter to resume scanning.<br />\n(c) Lexical fixes don&rsquo;t ensure syntactic validity; they only help scanning continue.<br />\n(d) Lookahead buffers help reduce premature or incorrect token recognition.<br />\ne.g. distinguish between similar tokens (like &ldquo;=&rdquo; vs &ldquo;==&rdquo;) and reduces misclassification.</p>"
  },
